   "narration_script": "A massive circular desert formation served as a visual reference for space shuttle crews. Near Ouadane on the Adrar Plateau of Mauritania, a subsurface igneous intrusion deformed overlying sedimentary rock layers into concentric rings framed by gabbroic dikes. Geologists initially considered it an extraterrestrial impact crater, until analysis in 1969 proved suspected shock coesite was mislabeled barite. Dating shows the carbonatite rocks cooled between 94 and 104 million years ago, long before prehistoric humans left exceptional accumulations of Acheulean stone tools. Deep erosion sculpted this natural dome into an unmistakable desert landmark seen from space."
